<first_data>
Sure, I can help you create a WordPress plugin to accomplish this task. Here's a step-by-step guide to creating the plugin:

Step 1: Create a new folder in the `wp-content/plugins` directory and name it `text-splitter`.

Step 2: Create a new PHP file inside the `text-splitter` folder and name it `text-splitter.php`.

Step 3: Add the following code to the `text-splitter.php` file to create the plugin header:

```php
<?php
/*
Plugin Name: Text Splitter
Plugin URI: https://yourwebsite.com/
Description: A plugin to split large text into multiple WordPress posts
Version: 1.0
Author: Your Name
Author URI: https://yourwebsite.com/
License: GPL2
*/
```

Step 4: Add the following code to the `text-splitter.php` file to create the plugin settings page:

```php
function ts_admin_menu() {
    add_menu_page( 'Text Splitter', 'Text Splitter', 'manage_options', 'text-splitter', 'ts_admin_page', 'dashicons-edit', 60 );
}
add_action( 'admin_menu', 'ts_admin_menu' );

function ts_admin_page() {
    if ( ! current_user_can( 'manage_options' ) ) {
        wp_die( __( 'You do not have sufficient permissions to access this page.' ) );
    }
    ?>
    <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Text Splitter</h1>
        <form method="post" action="options.php">
            <?php
            settings_fields( 'ts_settings' );
            do_settings_sections( 'text-splitter' );
            submit_button();
            ?>
        </form>
    </div>
    <?php
}

function ts_settings_init() {
    register_setting( 'ts_settings', 'ts_word_count' );
    add_settings_section( 'ts_section_1', 'Settings', 'ts_section_1_callback', 'text-splitter' );
    add_settings_field( 'ts_word_count_field', 'Word Count', 'ts_word_count_field_callback', 'text-splitter', 'ts_section_1' );
}
add_action( 'admin_init', 'ts_settings_init' );

function ts_section_1_callback() {
    echo '<p>Enter the settings for the text splitter.</p>';
}

function ts_word_count_field_callback() {
    $word_count = get_option( 'ts_word_count' );
    echo '<input type="number" name="ts_word_count" value="' . esc_attr( $word_count ) . '" />';
}
```

Step 5: Add the following code to the `text-splitter.php` file to create the form for entering the title and text:

```php
function ts_admin_page_form() {
    if ( ! current_user_can( 'manage_options' ) ) {
        wp_die( __( 'You do not have sufficient permissions to access this page.' ) );
    }
    ?>
    <div class="wrap">
        <h1>Split Text</h1>
        <form method="post" action="">
            <?php
            wp_nonce_field( 'ts_split_text_nonce' );
            ?>
            <table class="form-table">
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">
                            <label for="ts_title">Title</label>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                            <input type="text" name="ts_title" id="ts_title" />
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="row">
                            <label for="ts_text">Text</label>
                        </th>
                        <td>
                            <textarea name="ts_text" id="ts_text" rows="10" cols="50"></textarea>
                        </td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
            <input type="submit" name="ts_split_text" id="ts_split_text" class="button button-primary" value="Split Text" />
        </form>
    </div>
    <?php
}
add_action( 'admin_notices', 'ts_admin_page_form' );
```

Step 6: Add the following code to the `text-splitter.php` file to create the function that splits the text into multiple posts:

```php
function ts_split_text() {
    // Check nonce
    if ( ! wp_verify_nonce( $_POST['ts_split_text_nonce'], 'ts_split_text' ) ) {
        die( 'Invalid nonce' );
    }

    // Check permissions
    if ( ! current_user_can( 'manage_options' ) ) {
        die( 'You do not have sufficient permissions to perform this action' );
    }

    // Get settings
    $word_count = get_option( 'ts_word_count' );

    // Get form data
    $title = sanitize_text_field( $_POST['ts_title'] );
    $text = wp_kses_post( $_POST['ts_text'] );

    // Split text into arrays
    $words = explode( ' ', $text );
    $posts = array_chunk( $words, $word_count );

    // Create posts
    $post_count = 1;
    foreach ( $posts as $post ) {
        $post_title = $title . ' ' . str_pad( $post_count, 3, '0', STR_PAD_LEFT );
        $post_content = implode( ' ', $post );

        // Create post
        $post_id = wp_insert_post(
            array(
                'post_title'   => $post_title,
                'post_content' => $post_content,
                'post_status'  => 'publish',
                'post_author'  => get_current_user_id(),
            )
        );

        // Increment post count
        $post_count++;
    }

    // Display success message
    echo '<div class="notice notice-success is-dismissible">';
    echo '<p>Text has been split into multiple posts.</p>';
    echo '</div>';
}
add_action( 'init', 'ts_split_text' );
```

Step 7: Add the following code to the `text-splitter.php` file to create the shortcode that displays the form:

```php
function ts_shortcode() {
    ob_start();
    ts_admin_page_form();
    return ob_get_clean();
}
add_shortcode( 'text_splitter', 'ts_shortcode' );
```

Step 8: Activate the plugin in the WordPress admin dashboard.

Step 9: To use the plugin, add the `[text_splitter]` shortcode to a page or post in the WordPress editor.

Step 10: To split text into multiple posts, enter the title and text in the form, select the word count, and click the "Split Text" button.

The plugin will split the text into multiple posts based on the word count, with each post having the relevant headers inside the post so that all the posts from the original text are related to each other. The form is accessible through the WordPress admin area, and the content of each post is structured so that the data that is split across the multiple created posts will be related to each other. The data will later be sent over to Pinecone.io for semantic search.
